//RS // ── #2076: the run-scoped oracle-binary cache ───────────────────────────────
//RS //
//RS // Every dev battery pays an optimized (`-O`) rustc build per example, and the
//RS // six battery targets walk overlapping slices of the same corpus, so one full
//RS // dev run compiled the same binary up to ~958 times. That build is a pure
//RS // function of its inputs — the generated Rust, the toolchain, the flags, the
//RS // link inputs — so the second caller of one key can reuse the first caller's
//RS // artifact instead of paying for it again.
//RS //
//RS // `Tools/agent/verify-full.sh` gives every run a fresh disk-backed root via
//RS // `JET_DEV_ORACLE_CACHE_DIR`, shared by all six battery processes and removed
//RS // at run end. Ad-hoc runs get a process-scoped disk root below the same
//RS // gitignored scratch area; no developer cache crosses runs.
//RS //
//RS // The key covers everything that decides the bytes rustc emits, and every hit
//RS // is digest-verified. Everything else — a changed key input, a truncated
//RS // artifact, an unwritable root — is a MISS, and a miss is the old build path
//RS // unchanged.
//RS // Nothing here can turn a failing battery green: the cache sits strictly
//RS // between "generated Rust ready" and "binary on disk", and a failed build is
//RS // never published.
//RS 
//RS /// The oracle's rustc flags. ONE list, because it goes into the command AND
//RS /// into the cache key: a flag cannot enter the build without entering the key.
//RS const ORACLE_RUSTC_FLAGS: &[&str] = &["-O"];
//RS 
//RS /// What stopped an oracle build. The strict caller renders each case as the
//RS /// panic it always did, the `try_` caller reports every case as `None`, and
//RS /// neither one carries its own copy of the build (AGENTS.md I8).
//RS enum OracleBuildFailure {
//RS     Io(std::io::Error),
//RS     FrontEnd(Vec<jet::Diagnostics::Diagnostic>),
//RS     CLinks(Vec<jet::Diagnostics::Diagnostic>),
//RS     Rustc(Output),
//RS }
//RS 
