//RS /// Publish the freshly built `bin` as this entry's artifact, best effort.
//RS ///
//RS /// The artifact and its digest are staged in a private directory and the whole
//RS /// directory is renamed into place, so a reader can never meet an artifact whose
//RS /// digest has not landed yet. `ready` already existing means another process
//RS /// published this key first: same key, same inputs, nothing to do.
//RS ///
//RS /// A hard link, not a copy: the run then holds exactly ONE copy of the bytes per
//RS /// distinct oracle, whoever built it and however many batteries reuse it. The
//RS /// run-scoped root keeps that copy available to every battery in the run. A
//RS /// later build that writes over that same scratch path in place can change the
//RS /// published bytes — and that is caught rather than served, because a hit
//RS /// re-hashes the artifact against its recorded digest and a mismatch is a miss.
